<h2 class="content-box pad-box-mini" style="background-color: #941728; color: #ffffff;">Start with the idea of surprise</h2>
<p>Suppose a language model sees the prompt <strong>The capital of France is &hellip;</strong>. If it assigns a high probability to <strong>Paris</strong>, observing that token is not surprising. If it assigns a tiny probability to <strong>banana</strong> and that token nevertheless occurs, the result is much more surprising. This lesson turns that ordinary idea into four related measurements: <strong>self-information</strong> measures the surprise of one outcome; <strong>entropy</strong> measures the average surprise within one probability distribution; <strong>cross-entropy</strong> measures the average surprise when data from one distribution are evaluated using another; and <strong>KL divergence</strong> measures the extra surprise caused by using the second distribution.</p>
<p><strong>What you will do:</strong> calculate these quantities for small probability tables, then use the demonstration to see how a model is penalized when it assigns low probability to outcomes that actually occur.</p>
<p><strong>Video focus (under 10 minutes):</strong> surprise, expected surprise, cross-entropy, KL divergence, and their connection to negative log-likelihood.</p>
<div style="display: flex; align-items: center; margin: 0.75rem 0;">
<div style="flex: 1 1 auto; min-width: 0; text-align: center; overflow-x: auto;"><math style="font-size: 1.2rem;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block" aria-label="Equation 4.1. The self-information of outcome x equals negative log base two of p of x. The entropy of p equals the negative sum over x of p of x times log base two p of x."><mtable columnalign="right left" rowspacing="0.35em"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><mo>=</mo><mo>&minus;</mo><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mspace></mspace><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><mo>=</mo><mo>&minus;</mo><munder><mo>&sum;</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&isin;</mo><mi>𝒳</mi></mrow></munder><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mspace></mspace><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></math></div>
<span style="width: 3.5rem; flex: 0 0 3.5rem; text-align: right;" aria-hidden="true">(4.1)</span></div>
<p>Equation (4.1) uses a base-two logarithm, written log<sub>2</sub>, so the unit is <strong>bits</strong>. An outcome with probability 1/2 carries 1 bit of surprise; an outcome with probability 1/4 carries 2 bits. An event with probability 1 has zero surprise because &minus;log<sub>2</sub>(1) = 0. The uppercase <em>H</em> names entropy, and the &sum; symbol&mdash;<strong>sigma</strong>&mdash;means to average the surprise across all possible outcomes by adding the weighted terms. On a fixed finite set of outcomes, a uniform distribution has the greatest entropy because it is the least predictable.</p>
<h2 class="content-box pad-box-mini" style="background-color: #941728; color: #ffffff;">Cross-entropy and KL divergence</h2>
<p>In Equation (4.2), <em>p</em> is the distribution that describes the data or target, while <em>q</em> is the distribution supplied by the model. Cross-entropy asks how surprised model <em>q</em> is, on average, by outcomes weighted according to <em>p</em>. <strong>Kullback&ndash;Leibler divergence</strong>, usually shortened to <strong>KL divergence</strong>, measures the extra surprise compared with using <em>p</em> itself.</p>
<div style="display: flex; align-items: center; margin: 0.75rem 0;">
<div style="flex: 1 1 auto; min-width: 0; text-align: center; overflow-x: auto;"><math style="font-size: 1.2rem;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block" aria-label="Equation 4.2. The cross-entropy H of p comma q equals the negative sum over x of p of x times log base two q of x. The K L divergence from p to q equals the sum over x of p of x times log base two of p of x divided by q of x, which also equals cross-entropy minus entropy."><mtable columnalign="right left" rowspacing="0.35em"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><mo>=</mo><mo>&minus;</mo><munder><mo>&sum;</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&isin;</mo><mi>𝒳</mi></mrow></munder><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mspace></mspace><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>∥</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>&sum;</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&isin;</mo><mi>𝒳</mi></mrow></munder><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mspace></mspace><mfrac><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>&minus;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></math></div>
<span style="width: 3.5rem; flex: 0 0 3.5rem; text-align: right;" aria-hidden="true">(4.2)</span></div>
<p>The double bar in <em>D</em><sub>KL</sub>(<em>p</em> ∥ <em>q</em>) shows the direction from <em>p</em> to <em>q</em>; it is not the &ldquo;given&rdquo; bar used in conditional probability. Direction matters: switching <em>p</em> and <em>q</em> usually changes the answer. When <em>p</em> is fixed, its entropy <em>H</em>(<em>p</em>) is constant, so minimizing cross-entropy over <em>q</em> also minimizes KL divergence. KL divergence is therefore useful for comparing distributions, but it is not a geometric distance.</p>
<h2 class="content-box pad-box-mini" style="background-color: #941728; color: #ffffff;">Connection to model training</h2>
<p>For a one-hot target, categorical cross-entropy becomes the negative log probability assigned to the correct class or token. Averaging this quantity over training examples gives the negative log-likelihood objective used throughout machine learning.</p>
